  "body": "<!--\r\n⚠️ If you do not respect this template, your pull request will be closed.\r\n⚠️ Your pull request title should be short detailed and understandable for all.\r\n⚠️ Also, please add it in the CHANGELOG file under Unreleased section.\r\n⚠️ If your pull request fixes an open issue, please link to the issue.\r\n\r\n✅ I have added the tests to cover my changes.\r\n✅ I have updated the documentation accordingly.\r\n✅ I have read the CONTRIBUTING document.\r\n-->\r\n\r\n### Summary\r\n\r\nThis PR adds the following improvements to the `QubitMapper` classes:\r\n\r\n- [x] makes `suppress_none` a keyword-only argument everywhere (this matches how it was in the `QubitConverter`; this does not need a release note since it is an unreleased change)\r\n- [x] deprecates the `QubitMapper.allows_two_qubit_reduction` attribute (#1039)\r\n  - [x] needs to be added to the release note\r\n- [x] renames the `nmodes` argument to `register_length` everywhere\r\n  - [x] needs to be added to the release note\r\n\r\n### Details and comments\r\n\r\nThe reason for that last change is in preparation for #831. I do plan to include allowing to overwrite `register_length` as part of a call to `QubitMapper.map` in this PR. This already works mostly but I still need to check the mappers which do not use the `QubitMapper.mode_based_mapping`.\r\n\r\nEDIT: #831 is now fully implemented in this PR, too.\r\n\r\nFinally, I would also like to raise the question of whether we need to keep the `FermionicMapper`, `SpinMapper`, and `VibrationalMapper` classes. These do not really add any benefit except typing but those are not even enforced at runtime.\r\nI would thus suggest any of the following options:\r\n1. remove these classes alltogether (respecting the deprecation period ofcourse..)\r\n2. actually use these classes to enforce types at runtime (in the sense of an `isinstance` check in their `_map_single` methods and corresponding `super()._map_single(...)` calls in their subclasses\r\n3. at least hide these from the public API since they honestly just add noise to the documentation page of the mappers since these are abstract and cannot be used by the users directly anyways.\r\n\r\nEDIT: I opted to keep these classes but removed them from the public documentation page (since these are not actually usable by an end-user and add unnecessary noise). I updated the classes to type the public `.map` rather than private `._map_single` method such that an IDE can actually aid a user with these type hints.",
